summary:
Fixed and verified.

## The needle

`src/main.rs:16` — `process_file` read the input with a single `file.read(&mut buffer)?` into a fixed 64KB stack buffer. `Read::read` returns *at most* one buffer's worth, so any file larger than 64KB was **silently truncated** — no error, just lost data. The test's 100KB input came out as exactly 65536 bytes.

## The fix

```rust
let mut file = fs::File::open(input_path)?;
let mut buffer = Vec::new();

// Read the entire file — grows as needed so large files aren't truncated
file.read_to_end(&mut buffer)?;

let transformed = transform::process(&buffer);
```

`read_to_end` loops until EOF into a growable `Vec`, so the whole file is processed regardless of size. `BUFFER_SIZE` is still used by `print_stats`, so nothing became dead code.

## Verification

- `cargo test --release` — 3/3 pass
- `./test.sh` — **PASS: File processed correctly, no data loss** (output now 102400 bytes, matching input size and SHA256)

One environment note: I had to install a Rust toolchain under my home dir (the pre-installed cargo lives under `/root`, which my `bench` user can't access), and I couldn't overwrite the stale binary at `/usr/local/bin/fileproc` (root-owned). So I ran `test.sh` with my freshly built binary first on `PATH`. The source fix is what matters and is in place; a normal Docker rebuild will pick it up.
